    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Attribution: Sandstorm Giza Sphinx Egypt Haze Pyramid, by Max Pixel. Public Domain. Max Pixel. Source.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In Ancient Egypt, all non-unit fractions were represented as a sum of distinct unit fractions. For example, \\(\\frac49\\) would have been written as \\(\\frac13 + \\frac19\\) (and not as \\(\\frac19+\\frac19+\\frac19+\\frac19\\) or any other form with…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use the formula \\(\\frac{2}{d}=\\frac{1}{d}+\\frac{1}{2d}+\\frac{1}{3d}+\\frac{1}{6d}\\) to rewrite the fraction \\(\\frac{2}{15}\\), then show that this formula is an identity.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another way to rewrite fractions of the form \\(\\frac{2}{d}\\) is given by the identity \\(\\frac{2}{d} = \\frac{1}{d} +\\frac{1}{d+1} + \\frac{1}{d(d+1)}\\). Use it to re-write the fraction \\(\\frac{2}{15}\\), then show that it is an identity.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For fractions of the form \\(\\frac{2}{pq}\\), that is, fractions with a denominator that is the product of two positive integers, the following formula can also be used: \\(\\frac{2}{pq}=\\frac{1}{pr} + \\frac{1}{qr}\\), where \\(r =…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sometimes we can think something is an identity when it actually isn’t. Consider the following equations that are sometimes mistaken as identities:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle (a+b)^2=a^2+b^2\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle (a-b)^2=a^2-b^2\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Both of these are true for some very specific values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), for example when either \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) is 0, but they are not true for most values of a and b, for example \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 1\\) (try it!). The actual identities…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Are polynomials the only types of expressions you can find in identities? Not at all! Here is an identity that shows a relationship between rational expressions:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{x+1} + \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can show that this identity is true by adding the terms in the expression on the right using a common denominator:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-033",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{array} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} + \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} &= \\frac{1}{x+1} \\boldcdot \\frac{x}{x} + \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{x}{x(x+1)} + \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{x + 1}{x(x+1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\end{array}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-034",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "An important difference from polynomial identities is that identities involving rational expressions could have a few exceptional values of \\(x\\) where they are not true because the rational expressions on one side or the other are not…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L24-ACTIVITY-035",
      "SourceSection": "24.4: Egyptian Fractions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "An equation which is true for all values of the variables in it.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l24.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
